course: everyday
lesson: 92
title: Disappointment, jealousy and embarrassment
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Disappointment is the feeling when something you hoped for does not happen; naming it («I'm really disappointed») helps, and looking forward to the next chance helps even more.
explain: Jealousy is wanting what someone else has, or the attention they get; it is a normal feeling, but taking it out on others is not fair, and thinking about the good things you already have makes you happier.
explain: Embarrassment is the hot, red-faced feeling when you think everyone is looking at a mistake; but everyone trips, gets answers wrong and makes slips, and people forget much faster than you think.
explain: A boy in Dublin calls his teacher «Mum» in front of the class; he goes red, then laughs along, the teacher laughs too, and by lunchtime nobody remembers it.
fact: Saying you are disappointed helps, and planning for the next chance helps more.
fact: Many great players were not picked for a team at first and kept practising.
fact: When you get a present you did not want, say thank you warmly, because the giver thought of you.
fact: Jealousy is a normal feeling, but being mean because of it is not fair.
fact: Comparing yourself with others makes you unhappy, and being grateful for what you have makes you happier.
fact: Being happy for someone who did well makes you a good friend, and you can learn from them.
fact: A new baby needs a lot of care, but parents love an older child just as much.
fact: Everyone trips or makes mistakes sometimes, and people forget quickly.
fact: Mistakes in class are how everyone learns, and teachers want you to try.
fact: Calling a teacher «Mum» by mistake is one of the most common slips there is.
fact: If you have an accident like wetting yourself or being sick at school, tell a teacher quietly and they will help.
fact: When a classmate is embarrassed, do not laugh; say something kind or change the subject.
exercise: Lily's birthday party is cancelled because of rain. What is a good way to handle it? | Say she is disappointed, then help plan another day | Pretend she does not care about the party at all | Refuse to have any party ever again | Blame her parents for the bad weather
exercise: Why does naming a feeling like disappointment help? | It helps you understand the feeling and move on | It makes the rain stop sooner | It makes the party happen anyway | It hides the feeling from other people
exercise: Max is not picked for the school football team. What is the best response? | Ask the coach what to practise and keep trying | Give up football because he is clearly bad at it | Tell everyone the coach is unfair and stupid | Never try out for any team again
exercise: Grandma gives Zoe a jumper she does not like. What should Zoe do? | Say thank you warmly, because Grandma thought of her | Tell Grandma she would rather have had money | Say nothing and leave the present unopened | Tell Grandma it is the wrong kind of present
exercise: Your sister gets a new bike and you feel jealous. What is fair? | Tell a parent how you feel instead of being mean | Let her tyres down so it is fair | Tell her the bike is ugly and babyish | Refuse to speak to her for a week
exercise: You keep wishing you had a classmate's games console. What helps you feel happier? | Thinking about the good things you already have | Comparing your things with everyone else's | Asking for a new present every day | Telling the classmate that the console is rubbish
exercise: A classmate gets a better mark than you in spelling. What is a good thing to do? | Congratulate them and ask how they studied | Say they must have copied the answers | Ignore them for the rest of the day | Tell the teacher the marking was unfair
exercise: Since the new baby came, Jack feels nobody notices him. What should he do? | Ask a parent for some special time with just him | Be naughty on purpose so the adults notice him | Tell the baby that it should go away | Hide his feelings and keep out of the way
exercise: Why does a new baby get so much attention? | Babies need a lot of care, not more love | Parents love babies more than older children | The older child must have done something wrong | Babies are more important than big children
exercise: Mia trips over in front of the whole class. What is a good response? | Laugh it off, get up and carry on | Run out of the classroom and hide there | Shout at whoever was nearest to her | Refuse to come back to school tomorrow
exercise: Tom gives a wrong answer out loud and feels silly. What should he remember? | Mistakes are how everyone learns, and teachers want him to try | He should never answer a question in class again | Everyone in the class will remember it for years | Clever children never give wrong answers, so he must not be very clever
exercise: A boy calls his teacher «Mum» by mistake. What is the best thing to do? | Laugh along, because it happens to almost everyone | Pretend he said something else entirely | Stay silent and go red for the rest of the day | Ask to move to a different class
exercise: A girl is sick at school and feels very embarrassed. What should she do? | Tell a teacher quietly, who will help her clean up | Hide in the toilets until the end of the day so nobody sees her | Pretend nothing happened and carry on working | Go home by herself without telling anyone
exercise: A classmate goes red after dropping her tray in the canteen. What is kind? | Do not laugh; say something kind or change the subject | Laugh loudly, because that turns it into a joke for everyone, her too | Point it out so others can help her tidy up | Take a photo so she can laugh at it later
test: Ethan's camping trip is called off. Which response shows he handles disappointment well? | I'm disappointed, but let's pick another weekend | I don't care, camping is boring anyway | This always happens to me and never to anyone else | I'll stay angry until we go camping
test: Why is it a good idea to say thank you for a gift you do not like? | The giver spent thought, time or money on you | You might get a better present next time | Saying thank you means you must keep it forever | It is a rule only for birthdays
test: Which is true about jealousy? | It is a normal feeling, but we should not take it out on others | It is a bad feeling that only unkind people have | It goes away if you are mean to the other person | It shows that you deserve whatever it is the other person has got
test: A girl in Perth feels everyone is looking after she falls on the stage. What is most likely true? | People forget quickly, because everyone falls sometimes | Everyone will talk about it at school for the rest of the year | She will never be able to go on a stage again | Nobody else has ever fallen in front of people
test: Owen's friend wins the art prize Owen wanted. What would a good friend do? | Say well done and ask how the picture was made | Say the judges must have had favourites and the contest was unfair | Stop being friends with the winner | Pretend he never entered the contest
test: A boy wets himself on a school trip. What will a teacher most likely do? | Help him quietly, find clean clothes and keep it private | Tell the whole class so they understand | Make him sit apart from everyone else for the rest of the trip | Phone his parents and send him straight home
test: You were not picked for the choir. What thought helps most? | Lots of people are not picked at first; I can practise and try again | I am terrible at singing, so I will stop singing for good and never try again | The teacher must not like me at all | Choirs are silly and nobody wants to be in one
test: Which habit makes children happier in the long run? | Being grateful for what they have | Comparing their things with everyone else's | Wanting whatever their friends get | Keeping a list of what others have that they do not
test: Ruby's little brother gets many presents when he is born. Ruby feels jealous. What is the best thing she can do? | Tell her mum how she feels and ask for time together | Hide one of the baby's presents in her room so she has one too | Say the baby is boring and ugly | Keep away from her family until it passes
test: A classmate is laughed at after saying a wrong answer. What can you do? | Not join in, and say that it was a good try | Laugh too so you fit in with the others | Tell the teacher the answer was silly | Whisper the right answer to everyone else so they can laugh as well
test: didnt get picked for the team again, gonna quit footy | Feel sad, then keep practising for the next chance | Quit now, you will never get picked anyway so why bother | Tell the coach the team is rubbish | Stop playing anything at all
test: nan got me socks for my bday, ugh. what do i say | Thank you, warmly, she thought of you | I really wanted a computer game, not socks | Nothing, just put them down and walk off | Ask her for the money instead next time
test: called miss "mum" in class 😳 everyone heard, i wanna die | It is a very common slip and people forget fast | Everyone will remember it forever and laugh at u | Change schools so no one knows | Pretend you were joking on purpose
test: Ella tripped in assembly, everyones laughing, should i laugh too | No, say something kind or change the subject | Yes, everyone else is laughing | Yes, it was funny to watch | Copy her trip on purpose to cheer her up a bit
explain: A boy in Texas was jealous of his friend's new bike and let the air out of its tyres; he was found out, lost the friendship for weeks and had to pay for the repair.
explain: A girl in Dublin was so embarrassed after falling over on stage that she refused to go to school for three days; when she went back, she found almost no one had remembered.
fact: Acting on jealousy by spoiling someone else's things usually hurts the friendship and gets you in trouble.
fact: Most people forget an embarrassing moment of someone else's far sooner than the person it happened to.
test: jealous of my mate's new phone, wanna hide it on him | Don't, it hurts the friendship and gets you in trouble | Hide it, it's just a joke and he'll find it later anyway | Break it so you're even | Tell everyone it's stolen
test: tripped on stage, everyone saw, never going back to school | Go back, most people forget fast | Never go back, they'll remember forever | Change your name so nobody knows you | Hide in the toilets for the whole year
test: didn't make the team, gonna quit footy for good | It's ok to be upset, then keep practising and try again | Quit, you'll never be good enough to make the team anyway | Shout at the coach | Tell the team they're rubbish
test: did u hear Grace fell over on stage at the concert n her whole dress ripped n the WHOLE school saw, she'll never live it down | Everyone trips sometimes and people forget quickly; the ripped dress bit is likely exaggerated | She will be remembered for it forever by everyone who was at the concert | It is true, so she should change schools | She must have tripped on purpose for attention
test: someone said Leo scratched Max's new bike cos he was jealous, they're telling everyone | Do not spread it; it may not be true, and jealousy is normal but spoiling things is not | Spread it, jealous people deserve to be shown up in front of everyone | Spread it, but say you only heard it | Scratch Leo's bike back to make it fair
test: Ava called the teacher "Mum" in front of the whole class, the boys are saying it every day now | It is one of the commonest slips there is, so do not join the teasing | Join in, it is too funny to drop | Join in until she stops going red | Tell the teacher she does it on purpose just to get some attention
exam: Which of the following responses is appropriate upon the receipt of a present which one did not desire? | Thanking the giver warmly, since the giver thought of one | Informing the giver at once that the present is unwanted | Returning the present to the giver without any explanation | Giving the present away in the presence of the giver
exam: Identify the principal reason for which errors made during lessons ought not to occasion shame. | Mistakes are the means by which everyone learns, and teachers wish pupils to try | Mistakes are recorded permanently, so pupils ought never to attempt any answer | Mistakes are made only by those pupils who have failed to pay sufficient attention | Mistakes are entertaining for the class and should therefore be laughed at
exam: Pursuant to an accident such as being sick or wetting oneself at school, select the response consistent with the established facts. | The pupil informs a teacher quietly, who will assist | The pupil hides in the toilets until the end of the school day | The pupil leaves the school grounds without telling anyone | The pupil blames the classmate seated nearest to them
exam: In respect of jealousy, which statement most accurately characterises the established facts? | Jealousy is a normal feeling, but behaving unkindly because of it is not fair | Jealousy is an abnormal feeling which is experienced only by thoroughly unkind persons | Jealousy justifies the spoiling of another's possessions in order to restore fairness | Jealousy is best addressed by comparing oneself with others as often as possible
